course: adult-life
lesson: 28
title: Smoking, vaping and betting: habits that are hard to stop
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some habits hook people: they give a quick good feeling, then the body or mind wants that feeling again and again, until stopping becomes very hard.
explain: Nicotine, found in cigarettes and vapes, is one of these hooks; betting, where people risk money hoping to win more, is another.
explain: Picture Sunday in Melbourne: Grandad Ron has a nicotine patch on his arm and is snappy because he's giving up smoking, while Uncle Steve shouts at the footy on telly because he's put twenty dollars on the final score.
explain: A little flutter once a year is harmless fun for most people, but betting companies always win overall, and some people lose their homes and families to it.
explain: The easiest way never to have a hard habit is never to start one.
fact: Cigarettes contain tobacco and nicotine; smoking causes lung disease, heart disease and cancer, and it is banned inside workplaces and pubs, so smokers go outside even in the rain.
fact: Nicotine is very addictive, so smokers feel uncomfortable until they have the next one, and most started as teenagers.
fact: "Fags" is British slang for cigarettes, and a nicotine patch gives the body a little nicotine without the smoke to make quitting easier.
fact: Quitting smoking is one of the best things a person can do for their health; many people need several tries.
fact: Vaping means using an e-cigarette that heats a liquid with nicotine and flavouring; it is less harmful than smoking but not harmless, and still addictive.
fact: Vapes are made colourful and sweet-tasting, and selling them to under-18s is against the law.
fact: The lottery is a game where people buy a ticket and pick numbers; the chance of the jackpot is about one in 45 million, so almost everyone wins nothing.
fact: A bet is money risked on something happening, such as a score; if it happens you win more back, if not you lose your money.
fact: Betting companies set the odds so that, overall, they always make money.
fact: A scratch card is a quick lottery ticket; the "almost won" feeling is designed to make people buy another.
fact: "When the fun stops, stop" is a warning betting adverts must include, because gambling can become an addiction.
fact: A "flutter" is a small bet just for fun, such as a few pounds once a year on a famous horse race.
fact: Gambling addiction means a person keeps betting to win back losses, which leads to debt, lying and family arguments; help exists from doctors and charities.
fact: Gambling is against the law under 18, and a child should never keep a grown-up's gambling or money worries secret.
exercise: Why do office workers stand outside in the rain to smoke? | Smoking is not allowed inside workplaces | Smoke is good for them when it is raining | Their boss likes them to get wet | Cigarettes only light properly in fresh air
exercise: What makes cigarettes so hard to give up? | The nicotine in them is addictive | The paper is very tasty | They are very cheap to buy in every shop | They help people breathe better
exercise: Name an illness smoking can cause. | Lung cancer | Chickenpox | A broken leg | Short sight
exercise: Grandad has a sticky patch on his arm: "It's helping me give up the fags." What are "fags"? | Cigarettes | Sweets | Plasters | Biscuits
exercise: How does a nicotine patch help Grandad? | It gives him a little nicotine without the smoke | It cleans all the old smoke out of his lungs | It makes cigarettes taste of soap | It puts him to sleep whenever he wants a cigarette
exercise: Teenagers outside the shop are blowing big sweet-smelling clouds. What are they doing? | Vaping with e-cigarettes | Blowing bubbles with gum | Breathing out on a cold day | Using a smoke machine
exercise: Which is true about vaping? | It is still addictive and not harmless | It is completely safe, even for children | It contains no nicotine at all | It is just the same as breathing steam
exercise: Mum ticking numbers on a slip: "When we win the lottery, we'll get a pool." How likely is the jackpot? | Extremely unlikely, about one in 45 million | Very likely if she keeps playing every single week | About half the people who play win it | Certain if she picks her lucky numbers
exercise: Uncle at the match: "I've got a tenner on two-nil." What has he done? | Bet ten pounds the score will be exactly two-nil | Paid ten pounds for two match tickets | Promised ten pounds to whichever team wins the match | Bought two pies for ten pounds
exercise: Who makes money from betting overall? | The betting companies | Most of the people who bet | The football players | Nobody makes money from it
exercise: A grown-up at the counter scratches silver off a card with a coin. What is it? | A scratch card, a quick lottery ticket | A gift card for the shop | A bus pass being cleaned with a coin | A bank card with a new PIN
exercise: Why do scratch cards often "almost win"? | To make people want to buy another | Because the printers make mistakes | To give everyone a fair chance | Because the shop wants you to win
exercise: Football adverts say "When the fun stops, stop." Why? | Betting firms must warn that gambling can become an addiction | The match is about to end | It is a rule for the players, who must stop when the fun stops | The TV channel wants you to switch off
exercise: Gran on Grand National day: "I always have a little flutter on a horse." What does she mean? | She puts a small bet on the race for fun | She rides a horse once a year | She feels nervous about the race on the telly | She buys a horse every spring
exercise: His wife whispers: "He's lost so much money on those betting apps." What is the problem? | He has a gambling problem | His phone has a broken app | He gave money to charity | He forgot his bank password
exercise: Why are betting apps especially risky? | They are on the phone all day and night | They only let you win small amounts | They are free and need no money | They close at bedtime every night
exercise: Someone offers you a vape behind the shops. What do you say? | "No thanks" | "Just one try" | "Only the sweet one" | "Maybe when nobody's looking"
test: Your brother: "Vapes smell of strawberry so they're basically sweets." Put him right. | No, vapes have addictive nicotine; the flavour just tempts you | Yes, they are just sweets that you breathe in instead of eating | Yes, the fruit makes them healthy | No, vapes are made only of water
test: Grandad's been snapping at everyone all week. Mum: "Go easy, he's on day five without a ciggie." Why is he snappy? | His body is missing nicotine while he quits | He is cross with you about your room | He has caught a bad cold | He wants more presents from the family at Christmas
test: Mum: "Lottery's up to 100 million this week, I've got a good feeling!" Which plan is wiser for buying a pool? | Saving a bit of money every month | Buying lots of tickets every week | Choosing the numbers of her birthday | Waiting until she has a lucky dream
test: Uncle Steve, fuming: "Bloody hell, 2–1! There goes my twenty quid." What happened? | He bet twenty pounds on a different score and lost | He won twenty pounds on the match | Someone stole twenty pounds at the match | He paid twenty pounds to get into the match
test: Overheard: "I won a fiver on a scratchie, so I bought six more." Over a month, what is most likely? | She spends far more than she wins | She wins much more than she spends | She wins every single time | She breaks even exactly
test: Gran gives you a newspaper page to pick a horse "with a funny name, no money, just for fun." Is that gambling? | No, there is no money involved, it is just a game | Yes, it is against the law for children to play | Yes, Gran will lose her house | No, because horses cannot be bet on by anybody at all
test: Aunt Kerry, crying on the phone: "He swore he'd stopped, but he's been betting on his phone at 3 a.m. and the rent's gone." What does Uncle need? | Help for a gambling addiction | A new phone with a bigger screen | A luckier betting app | More money to win the rent back
test: Your friend says: "My dad keeps betting and Mum's crying about money. Don't tell anyone." What should you do? | Tell a trusted adult, because this is not a secret to keep | Keep the secret for ever | Give your friend your pocket money so he can win it back | Tell the whole class at lunch
test: Josh, 15, is offered a cigarette: "Go on, one won't hurt, you can stop whenever." What is most true? | Many smokers started as teenagers and could not stop | Teenagers cannot get addicted to nicotine | One cigarette makes you a smoker for life | Cigarettes are safe until you are 30
test: A bookie's advert shows smiling winners every football break. What does it never show? | The many people who lost money | The football match itself | The name of the betting company | Any people at all
test: Which of these is a "flutter"? | Two pounds on a horse once a year | Betting the rent money every week | Playing on a betting app every night | Borrowing money to keep betting
exam: Identify the substance contained in both cigarettes and electronic cigarettes which renders them addictive. | Nicotine | Caffeine | Water vapour | Sugar
exam: It is given that betting undertakings determine the odds offered to their customers. Ascertain who, in aggregate, derives financial benefit therefrom. | The betting undertakings | The majority of persons placing bets | The sporting teams concerned | The government exclusively
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the expression "a little flutter". | A small wager placed for amusement | A severe addiction to gambling | A feeling of nervousness before a race | A loan obtained in order to gamble
exam: Determine which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the likelihood of winning a national lottery jackpot. | The probability is approximately one in forty-five million | The probability exceeds one in two for regular players | The probability is certain for persons choosing birthdays | The probability increases after each loss
